  "problemStatement": {
    "formal": "For a spherical triangle with vertices represented by unit vectors A, B, C, define cevian points on the opposite arcs by normalized positive linear combinations, for example D = (α_D B + β_D C) / ‖α_D B + β_D C‖ on arc BC. The intended result is that sin(BD) / sin(DC) = β_D / α_D for one cevian, the product of the three corresponding sine ratios equals the product of the weight ratios, and the three cevians are concurrent exactly when α_D α_E α_F = β_D β_E β_F.",
    "plain": "The spherical Ceva theorem expressed concretely using unit vectors in an inner product space. Cevian points on arcs are defined by weight parameters (α, β) via normalization of α·B + β·C. The key results: sin(BD)/sin(DC) = β/α for a single cevian, the triple product of sin-ratios equals the weight-product ratio, and the spherical Ceva concurrency condition reduces to α_D·α_E·α_F = β_D·β_E·β_F.",
    "whyMatters": [
      "It recasts spherical Ceva in concrete inner-product and unit-vector terms.",
      "The weighted-normalization formulation connects geometric arc ratios to algebraic parameter equalities."
    ]
  },
